"And what about spicy food? Spicy is not a taste. The chemical in chili peppers switches on nerves that feel pain and heat, the same nerves that warn you about soup that is too hot. That is why spicy food “burns.” Mint plays the opposite trick: it switches on nerves that feel cold. Your brain adds these feelings to taste and smell, along with the crunch and the warmth of the food, and the result is the flavor of your lunch. Now find a partner and try the pinch test below the model.": "3cdac7c9c7ce",
"How do smells from the food in your mouth reach your nose?": "ff9facecdf7d",
"Is this hot pot spicy?": "3779b445144d",
"Look at your tongue in a mirror. The little bumps are called papillae, and they are not taste buds. The taste buds are hidden in the sides of many of those bumps, and they are much too small to see. You have several thousand of them: most are on your tongue, and some are on the roof of your mouth and in your throat. Each taste bud is a tiny bundle of taste cells with a small opening at the top. When food dissolves in your saliva, its molecules wash into that opening, and the taste cells send a signal along nerves to your brain. Taste cells wear out fast, so your body replaces them about every ten days. That is why a burned tongue feels normal again so soon.": "1ffabb17a7c9",
"Pinch your nose and take a bite.": "e423d59b4586",
"Pinch your nose shut and pop a fruit candy into your mouth. It is sweet, and maybe a little sour, but which fruit is it? You cannot tell. Now let go of your nose. Strawberry! What happened? The thing you call “taste” is really two senses working together. Your tongue does one part of the job, your nose does the other, and your brain mixes them into one thing: flavor.": "aa26f4d6f0b6",
"Pinch your nose shut and pop a fruit candy into your mouth. It is sweet, and maybe a little sour, but which fruit is it? You cannot tell. Now let go of your nose. Strawberry! What happened? The thing you call “taste” is really two senses working together. Your tongue does one part of the job, your nose does the other, and your brain mixes them into one thing: flavor. Look at your tongue in a mirror. The little bumps are called papillae, and they are not taste buds. The taste buds are hidden in the sides of many of those bumps, and they are much too small to see. You have several thousand of them: most are on your tongue, and some are on the roof of your mouth and in your throat. Each taste bud is a tiny bundle of taste cells with a small opening at the top. When food dissolves in your saliva, its molecules wash into that opening, and the taste cells send a signal along nerves to your brain. Taste cells wear out fast, so your body replaces them about every ten days. That is why a burned tongue feels normal again so soon. Taste cells can report only five basic tastes: sweet, sour, salty, bitter, and umami. Scientists think each one is a useful message. Sweet means sugar, and sugar means energy. Salty means minerals your body needs. Sour means acid, as in a lemon or in fruit that is not ripe. Bitter is a warning, because many poisons are bitter. Umami is the savory taste of broth, mushrooms, and cheese, and it tells you that a food has protein. It was the last of the five to be named: in 1908, the Japanese chemist Kikunae Ikeda found it in kelp broth. You may have seen a map of the tongue that puts sweet at the tip, sour and salty on the sides, and bitter at the back. The map is wrong. Every part of your tongue that has taste buds can sense all five tastes. The mistake is more than eighty years old. In 1901, a German scientist measured very small differences from one part of the tongue to another. In 1942, an American textbook drew those results as a simple chart, and readers took it to mean that each area senses just one taste. You can test this yourself with a little sugar: you will taste it wherever it touches. So where does “strawberry” come from? From your nose. High inside it is a small patch of smell cells, with about 400 kinds of smell sensors. Every smell switches on its own mix of sensors, like a chord played on a piano, and that is how you can tell thousands of smells apart. Smells reach the patch by two roads. The front door is your nostrils: you sniff. The back door is a passage that joins the top of your throat to your nose. While you chew, smell molecules from the food float up through this back door. Pinch your nose, or catch a cold, and that air cannot move. The food seems to have almost no flavor at all. And what about spicy food? Spicy is not a taste. The chemical in chili peppers switches on nerves that feel pain and heat, the same nerves that warn you about soup that is too hot. That is why spicy food “burns.” Mint plays the opposite trick: it switches on nerves that feel cold. Your brain adds these feelings to taste and smell, along with the crunch and the warmth of the food, and the result is the flavor of your lunch. Now find a partner and try the pinch test below the model.": "b471c825c083",
